ws and pair programming are carried out on most of the project\u0027s code."},{"v":"Significant"},null,{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},{"v":1.5,"f":"1,5"},{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},null,{"v":null}]},{"c":[null,{"v":"6.3"},{"v":"Is there an effective CI/CD process?"},{"v":"Having a CI/CD is a good indicator of how easily the team will be able to work on the application with confidence."},null,{"v":"The criterion is met if projects are regularly built and tested automatically via a CI/CD tool."},{"v":"Significant"},null,{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},{"v":1.5,"f":"1,5"},{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},null,{"v":null}]},{"c":[null,{"v":"6.4"},{"v":"Are there non-regression tests (unit tests, integration tests, end-to-end tests)?"},{"v":"Having non-regression tests lets developers change the software while being assured that their work causes no problems."},null,{"v":"The criterion is met if the project has a complete set of automated tests covering the most important parts of the source code."},{"v":"Decisive"},null,{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},{"v":2.0,"f":"2"},{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},null,{"v":null}]},{"c":[null,{"v":"6.5"},{"v":"Is there strong decoupling between the business domain and the technical integration?"},{"v":"Decoupling practices such as dependency injection or hexagonal/clean/onion architecture enable more effective and ambitious technical changes.\nThis is the \"D\" (dependency inversion) of the 5 fundamental SOLID principles."},null,{"v":"The criterion is met if the product\u0027s technical aspects can be changed without changing the functional code."},{"v":"Significant"},null,{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},{"v":1.5,"f":"1,5"},{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},null,{"v":null}]},{"c":[null,{"v":"6.6"},{"v":"Are there indicators for tracking software quality?"},{"v":"Such as code coverage, cyclomatic complexity, number of issues, number of deployment issues, number of rollbacks, lead time for changes, deployment frequency, change failure rate, recovery time after a failed deployment."},null,{"v":"The criterion is met if software quality metrics are in place."},{"v":"Decisive"},null,{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},{"v":2.0,"f":"2"},{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},null,{"v":null}]},{"c":[null,{"v":"6.7"},{"v":"Is there comprehensive documentation?"},{"v":"Relevant documentation makes more ambitious changes possible."},null,{"v":"The criterion is met if the project has clear, usable documentation."},{"v":"Significant"},null,{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},{"v":1.5,"f":"1,5"},{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},null,{"v":null}]},{"c":[null,{"v":"6.8"},{"v":"Is there duplicated code in the application?"},{"v":"Duplicated code can be removed to simplify product maintenance."},null,{"v":"The criterion is met if there is no unjustified duplicated code in the project."},{"v":"Moderate"},null,{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},{"v":1.0,"f":"1"},{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},null,{"v":null}]},{"c":[null,{"v":"6.9"},{"v":"Does the team have enough autonamey to deploy the tools it needs to observe, measure, or optimize the system?"},{"v":"Ease of change increases when teams can rely on the tools they need to monitor performance, measure impact, or analyze behavior.\nThis autonamey can come from being able to install custom tools, or from relying on comprehensive built-in tools provided by the platform.\nWhat matters is not where the infrastructure is hosted, but the team\u0027s ability to use it effectively to support optimization."},null,{"v":"The criterion is met if the team can use or deploy the tools it needs to observe, measure, and optimize the system."},{"v":"Moderate"},null,{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},{"v":1.0,"f":"1"},{"v":0.0,"f":"0"},null,{"v":null}]},{"c":[null,{"v":"6.10"},{"v":"Are the main components subject to high-availability constraints?"},{"v":"High-availability requirements can reduce the team\u0027s ability to modify, migrate, or optimize components.\nServices that must maintain strict availability leave fewer opportunities for maintenance windows, architectural changes, or resource optimization.\nUnderstanding these constraints helps assess how easily the system can evolve toward a lower enviro